<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Manage a Club – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage a Club</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Develop a notice that clearly conveys information. Interactive: Participate in a written discussion on paper, through apps, online, or in a forum discussion.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short notice to recruit club members that can be shared in any classroom (e.g., "Join our art club! We meet weekly to create projects together.").
            Can respond to a question in a forum about club preferences within classroom settings by providing meaningful reasons (e.g., "I chose the environmental club because we can help reduce waste by organizing a school-wide recycling program." "I joined the debate club because it helps me improve my critical thinking and public speaking skills.").
            Can write a brief post describing club goals and activities that can happen in flexible spaces (e.g., "Our social club meets to share ideas and help each other.").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, and introducing the lesson's writing focus through clear modeling and concept checking.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of various school clubs (e.g., a debate team, an environmental club, an art club, a sports team). Students are asked to quickly write down 2-3 sentences about which club they would like to join and why, using the simple present tense to describe their preferences. (e.g., "I like the environmental club because we can help reduce waste.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple, concise notice for a hypothetical club, emphasizing key information and the use of imperatives. For example, the teacher writes on the board: "Join our NEW Robotics Club! We meet every Friday to build amazing projects. Develop leadership and teamwork skills. All members are welcome! Come to Room 301. Let's create!" The teacher highlights the target vocabulary (join, meet, project, leadership, teamwork, members, create) and pronunciation-focused spelling (e.g., 'pr' in 'project', 't' in 'team', '-ate' ending in 'create').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main purpose of this notice?" (To recruit members for a club).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which words tell you what to do?" (Join, Come, Let's create – pointing to imperatives).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduction of new language and content related to the scenario "Let's Start a Club!", specifically focusing on examples of written communication.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents various authentic written examples related to clubs: a short notice, a section of an online forum discussion about club preferences, and a brief social media post describing club goals and activities. These examples will contain target vocabulary and grammatical structures (simple present, imperatives, simple past). For instance, an example forum post might include: "Last year, I joined a sports club, and I made new friends. This year, I want to join a collaborative club. What type of club would you like to join?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Handout with three distinct written texts: a club recruitment notice (e.g., "Join our Eco-Adventures club! We organize clean-up events and learn about environmental leadership. Meetings are in the science lab."), a forum discussion excerpt (e.g., "UserA: What type of club would you like to join? UserB: I chose the debate club because it helps me improve my critical thinking and public speaking skills."), and a social media post (e.g., "Our social club meets to share ideas and help each other. We plan exciting events for the community!"). These texts feature nouns like <i>members, goals, activities, project, event, community, leadership</i>; verbs like <i>organize, plan, meet, improve, share</i>; and adjectives like <i>environmental, critical, public, social</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Develop a notice that clearly conveys information. Participate in a written discussion through apps, online, or in a forum discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the texts and answer gist questions: "What is the main idea of each text?" and "Who is the intended audience for each text?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete a True/False exercise based on specific details in the texts. E.g., "The Eco-Adventures club meets in the library. (T/F)".</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities to practice accuracy in writing, focusing on target grammar and vocabulary for the "Let's Start a Club!" scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences and short paragraphs using simple present (describing clubs, preferences), imperatives (instructions), and simple past (narrating experiences). Emphasis on target vocabulary: <i>clubs, interests, activities, goals, members, requirements, responsibilities, meetings, team, project, event, community, suggestions, participation, leadership, manage, organize, create, engage, discuss, respond, coordinate, support, inclusive, goal-oriented, collaborative</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> Students receive a paragraph about starting a club with blanks. They must fill in the blanks using a word bank containing target vocabulary (e.g., "Our new [club] needs [members]. We want to [plan] exciting [activities]. Let's [set goals] together."). This activity also includes sentences requiring simple present or imperatives.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Guided Sentence/Paragraph Writing):</b> Students are given prompts to write short sentences or combine sentences.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A. Write two imperative sentences inviting people to a club (e.g., "Join our debate team! Improve your critical thinking skills.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B. Write two sentences using the simple present to describe a club's activities (e.g., "Our club meets weekly to organize events. We create projects for the community.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">C. Write a brief sentence using the simple past to describe a past club experience (e.g., "Last year, I participated in the art club and created a mural.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A writes a short "forum question" asking about club preferences (e.g., "What kind of club do you want to join, and what activities would you like to participate in?"). Partner B writes a response, including reasons and using simple present and target vocabulary. Then they switch roles. After writing, partners exchange papers and check each other's writing for correct grammar, appropriate vocabulary, and clarity, providing constructive feedback.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the target language to produce a complete written product in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and communicative task completion.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will individually create two written products based on the "Let's Start a Club!" scenario:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Club Recruitment Notice:</b> Students write a short notice (50-70 words) for a new school club they wish to start. The notice must clearly convey information, include a call to action (imperatives), describe club goals and activities (simple present), and mention requirements for members. (e.g., "Join our Tech Innovators Club! We meet in the computer lab to create exciting projects, manage new technologies, and develop leadership skills. Our goal is to support collaborative learning. No prior experience required! Come participate every Tuesday across from the library. Let's make a difference!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Forum Post Response:</b> Students write a short response (40-60 words) to a given forum question about club preferences or roles (e.g., "What roles and responsibilities would you like to negotiate if you joined a club, and why?"). Their response must provide meaningful reasons, using target vocabulary (e.g., "I would like to be the treasurer because I am good at math and can help manage funds for events. I also enjoy organizing team projects and contributing to the community."). Students can also narrate a short story using the simple past if relevant (e.g., "Last year, I delegated tasks in my school project, and it helped us achieve our goals.").</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support and guidance, focusing on the clarity of the message, appropriate use of grammar, and vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' written work and opportunity for self-reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist/rubric to assess the written notices and forum posts. The focus is on the communicative success of the writing tasks.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written club recruitment notice and forum post response. The teacher collects them for a quick review.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and completeness of information in the notice (10 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>goals, activities, members, manage, organize, contribute</i>) (5 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of simple present, imperatives, and simple past (5 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Coherence and organization of ideas (5 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Meaningful reasons provided in the forum post (5 points)</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your club notice or forum response?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary words or grammar structures did you use effectively today?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about writing a short notice to recruit club members?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned today that will help you communicate better in writing about clubs?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pair work for the forum post writing was effective for practicing interactive written communication and providing peer feedback. Students demonstrated a good grasp of imperatives and simple present in their notices."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Some students struggled with integrating the simple past naturally when narrating stories in the forum posts. I need to provide more scaffolded examples or sentence starters for past experiences in the next lesson's writing component."</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their club ideas and present them, integrating the writing skills practiced today into a larger project involving summarizing and communicating information about their proposed clubs. This will require them to consolidate the information from their written notices and forum posts.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>